    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. Salmond's Five Characteristics of a Legal Right</h3>\n<p>Sir John Salmond posited that every legal right involves five indispensable elements:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Person of Inherence (Subject of the Right):</strong> The person in whom the right is vested; the beneficiary or owner of the right.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Person of Incidence (Subject of the Duty):</strong> The person upon whom the correlative duty falls; the person bound to respect the right.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Content or Substance:</strong> The act or forbearance which the person of incidence is obliged to perform or observe in favor of the person of inherence.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Object of the Right (Res):</strong> The physical thing, intangible interest, or state of affairs over which the right is exercised (e.g., land, reputation, patent).</li>\n  <li><strong>The Title:</strong> The legal vestitive fact or event by which the right was acquired (e.g., contract, purchase, gift, inheritance, prescription).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>2. Theories of Legal Rights: Will Theory vs. Interest Theory</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Will Theory (Savigny, Puchta, Austin, Holland):</strong> Defines a right as the inherent power or dominion of the human will. A legal right is an area of autonomous personal liberty protected by law. <em>Criticism:</em> Incapable of explaining rights granted to infants, unborn persons, mentally incapacitated persons, or animals, who lack conscious legal will.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Interest Theory (Rudolf von Ihering, Salmond):</strong> Defined by Ihering as a <em>'legally protected interest'</em>. Rights are not created to exalt the will, but to satisfy basic human and social needs. The law protects substantial economic, physical, or personal interests regardless of whether the right-holder possesses capacity to exercise conscious will.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Correlation Between Rights and Duties: The Absolute Duty Debate</h3>\n<p>Austin posited that while all rights entail duties, certain duties are <strong>Absolute Duties</strong> which have no corresponding rights:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Duties owed to the Sovereign/State (e.g., paying taxes, not committing treason).</li>\n  <li>Duties owed to persons indefinite (public at large, e.g., duty not to create a public nuisance).</li>\n  <li>Self-regarding duties (e.g., prohibition of suicide, duty not to commit self-harm).</li>\n  <li>Duties owed to non-human entities (e.g., duty not to be cruel to animals).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Salmond and Holland strongly rejected Austin's view:</em> Salmond maintained that every legal duty must have a corresponding right vested in someone (even if vested collectively in the State representing the public), because a duty without a right is a conceptual impossibility.</p>\n",
