    "learningObjective": "Distinguish between Right in Rem and Right in Personam, Proprietary and Personal rights, Perfect and Imperfect rights, and Vested and Contingent rights.",
    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. Right in Rem vs. Right in Personam</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Right in Rem (Real Right):</strong> Available against the entire world at large (<em>contra mundum</em>). It corresponds to a general negative duty incumbent on every person not to interfere. Examples: Right to ownership of land, right to bodily safety, right to reputation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Right in Personam (Personal Right):</strong> Available only against a specific, determinate person or group of persons. It usually corresponds to a positive duty to do something. Example: Right of a creditor against a borrower for repayment of a debt; right of a promisee under a contract.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Proprietary vs. Personal Rights</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Proprietary Rights:</strong> Rights constituting a person's estate, assets, or wealth. They have economic value and are generally transferable and alienable. Examples: Land, shares, patents, actionable claims.</li>\n  <li><strong>Personal Rights:</strong> Rights pertaining to a person's status, personal liberty, or bodily integrity. They have no commercial value and are inalienable. Examples: Right to life, freedom of speech, right to vote.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Perfect vs. Imperfect Rights</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Perfect Right:</strong> Recognized by law and backed by an immediate direct judicial remedy (enforceable in a court of law).</li>\n  <li><strong>Imperfect Right:</strong> Recognized by law but lacking a direct action for enforcement. Example: A debt barred by the Law of Limitation (the debt is not extinguished; it cannot be sued upon, but if paid voluntarily or retained through lien, the payment is lawful).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Principal vs. Accessory Rights</h3>\n<p>A Principal right exists independently on its own merit (e.g., debt). An Accessory right is parasitic and dependent upon a principal right (e.g., mortgage, pledge, or personal guarantee securing the debt).</p>\n",
