    "learningObjective": "Compare civil and criminal administration of justice, and master the 5 primary theories of punishment: Deterrent, Preventive, Retributive, Reformative, and Expiatory.",
    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. The Administration of Justice: Salmond's Primary Function of the State</h3>\n<p>Salmond defined the administration of justice as <strong>'the maintenance of right within a political community by means of the physical force of the state.'</strong> The state replaces private self-help and vengeance with an impartial judicial tribunal and institutionalized public force.</p>\n<p><strong>Civil vs. Criminal Justice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Civil Justice:</strong> Enforces a primary right or awards pecuniary compensation/restitution to the injured plaintiff (Redressal of private wrongs).</li>\n  <li><strong>Criminal Justice:</strong> Imposes penal sanctions (fines, imprisonment, forfeiture) upon the offender to deter crime and vindicate public order (Punishment of public wrongs).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Five Theories of Punishment</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Deterrent Theory (Jeremy Bentham, Cesare Beccaria):</strong> The primary object of punishment is to deter the offender from repeating the crime (Specific Deterrence) and to serve as an exemplary warning to others (General Deterrence). Bentham's utilitarian hedonistic calculus: <em>'The pain of punishment must outweigh the anticipated pleasure of the crime.'</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Preventive Theory (Paton, Holmes):</strong> Punishment seeks to disable the offender physically from committing further crimes, by imprisonment, suspension of license, deportation, or capital punishment. Salmond: <em>'It prevents the offender from offending again.'</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Retributive Theory (Immanuel Kant, Hegel):</strong> Rooted in lex talionis (<em>'An eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth'</em>). Punishment is an end in itself — a moral necessity to restore the cosmic equilibrium of justice disturbed by crime. Kant's categorical imperative: even if civil society were to dissolve tomorrow, the last murderer in prison must be executed to satisfy moral justice.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reformative / Rehabilitative Theory:</strong> Crime is a disease resulting from social, psychological, or economic maladjustment. The prison must become a hospital or reformatory school. Focus is on rehabilitation, probation, parole, and vocational education. (<em>'Hate the crime, not the criminal'</em>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Expiatory Theory:</strong> Grounded in ancient moral expiation. When an offender suffers punishment voluntarily, his guilt is washed away through suffering and remorse.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Modern Sentencing Philosophy in India</h3>\n<p>Indian judicial philosophy synthesizes <strong>Deterrence and Reformation</strong>. As held in <em>Sunil Batra</em> and <em>Bachan Singh</em>, reformation is the primary objective of correctional custody, while capital punishment is strictly reserved for the <strong>'rarest of rare cases'</strong> where the alternative option of life imprisonment is unquestionably foreclosed.</p>\n",
    "quickRevisionPoints": [
      "Salmond: Administration of justice = maintenance of right by the physical force of the state.",
      "Civil justice = Redressal/compensation; Criminal justice = Penal sanction/punishment.",
      "Deterrent Theory (Bentham): Make punishment an example; pain outweighs pleasure.",
      "Preventive Theory: Physical incapacitation (imprisonment, forfeiture, death).",
      "Retributive Theory (Kant, Hegel): Lex talionis; punishment is a categorical moral necessity.",
      "Reformative Theory: Treat crime as a disease; prison as a hospital for rehabilitation.",
      "Indian doctrine: Synthesis of reform and deterrence; death penalty restricted to 'Rarest of Rare'."
    ],
